introduction:
  bigQuestion: "Can we build a brain?"
  overview: "This unit explores AI that copies nature. You will investigate <strong>Reinforcement Learning</strong> (Training a dog with treats), <strong>Genetic Algorithms</strong> (Evolution and Survival of the Fittest), and <strong>Neural Networks</strong> (Mimicking the human brain's neurons)."
  journey: "You'll start by looking at <strong>Agents</strong> that learn from their environment. Then you'll simulate evolution with <strong>Genetic Algorithms</strong>. Finally, you'll look inside the 'Black Box' of <strong>Deep Learning</strong> and Convolutions (CNNs)."

concepts:
  - title: "Reinforcement Learning (RL)"
    icon: "fa-robot"
    content: |
      <p>Learning by Trial and Error. Used in Robotics and Gaming.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Agent:</strong> The AI interacting with the world.</li>
          <li><strong>Environment:</strong> The world itself.</li>
          <li><strong>Reward:</strong> The feedback (+1 for good, -1 for bad).</li>
          <li><strong>Policy:</strong> The strategy the agent learns to maximize rewards.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Genetic Algorithms (GA)"
    icon: "fa-dna"
    content: |
      <p>Solving problems by evolving solutions over generations.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Fitness Function:</strong> Decides who survives (Survival of the Fittest).</li>
          <li><strong>Crossover:</strong> Mixing parents to create children.</li>
          <li><strong>Mutation:</strong> Random changes to prevent getting stuck.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Neural Networks (ANN)"
    icon: "fa-network-wired"
    content: |
      <p>Mathematical models inspired by biological brains.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Neuron:</strong> A math function that takes inputs, multiplies by weights, and fires.</li>
          <li><strong>Hidden Layers:</strong> The "thinking" layers between input and output.</li>
          <li><strong>Backpropagation:</strong> The learning process (correcting errors).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)"
    icon: "fa-eye"
    content: |
      <p>Special AI for Vision (Images).</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Convolution:</strong> Scanning an image with filters to find edges/features.</li>
          <li><strong>Pooling:</strong> Shrinking the image to focus on what matters.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Agent"
    definition: "An autonomous entity that perceives its environment and acts upon it."
  - term: "Reward Signal"
    definition: "Feedback from the environment that tells the agent how well it is performing."
  - term: "Fitness Function"
    definition: "A function that quantifies how close a given solution is to the optimum goal."
  - term: "Crossover"
    definition: "Combining genetic information of two parents to generate new offspring."
  - term: "Mutation"
    definition: "A random alteration in the genetic structure of an offspring."
  - term: "Neural Network"
    definition: "A network of artificial neurons used to solve complex problems."
  - term: "Weight"
    definition: "A parameter within a neural network that transforms input data within the network's hidden layers."
  - term: "Convolution"
    definition: "A mathematical operation that slides a filter over an input (image) to detect features."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "RL needs Teachers"
    correction: "Reinforcement Learning is NOT Supervised. There is no 'Correct Answer' key. The agent just knows if it got a treat or a shock."
  - mistake: "Mutation is Bad"
    correction: "In Biology, maybe. In AI, Mutation is CRITICAL. Without it, the algorithm gets stuck in one solution (Local Optima)."
  - mistake: "Neural Nets 'Think'"
    correction: "They don't think. They are just giant multiplication machines fitting a curve to data."

